// Input. `readByte()` is the next byte of the standard input, or -1 at its end, and `String::read(limit)` and `String::readLine(limit)` read a
// String of up to `limit` bytes (a line without its new line). A value that is read is not known to the validator beyond its range (-1 to 255 for
// a byte, at most `limit` bytes for a String), so what a program relies on it has to test. The limit is how a loop over input still ends.
// This writes its input in capitals and returns how many bytes it had (0 when it is run with nothing to read).

text = String::read(1000);
n = size(text);

i = 0;
while i < n [{ affirm i >= 0; affirm i <= n; affirm size(text) = n; }] shrinks n - i {
    c = get(text, i);
    if c >= 97 {
        if c <= 122 {
            text = set(text, i, c - 32);
        };
    };
    i = i + 1;
};

text.print();
return n;
